Title: No-forge backend cannot list open review requests
Created: 2026-11-05
Author: Minh Ha-Duong

--- log ---
2026-11-05T15:18Z Minh Ha-Duong created
2026-11-07T18:49Z reviewer note Imagine/Plan: strict notes snapshot listing, explicit per-request abandon event, pinned-head ancestry, no inferred abandonment; mixed native/adopted PR control, malformed vs empty exit contract. Full remote-less dashboard adaptation stays out of scope and limitation goes to1445.

--- body ---

## Context

On a repository without a forge (`--forge none`, or no origin remote) there
is no way to ask "which review requests are open?".
`scripts/forge.py --forge none list --state open` exits 3 ("no forge;
nothing was read"), because `NoneBackend.list_requests` returns NOOP
(`scripts/forge.py`, around line 624). `resolve branch:<name>` answers for a
branch you already know about. Otherwise you have to piece the list together
by hand from unmerged branches and their latest request in `refs/notes/review`
(`scripts/review_notes.py`).

This matters more now that Dyne is closed (git-erg PR #380). Git notes are the
review store, and remote-less repositories exist (arena on padme,
matrice-hybride on doudou: survey of 2026-11-05). `/dashboard` and any
open-request sweep see nothing there.

Branch ancestry alone cannot tell an open request from a rejected or
abandoned one (git-erg `docs/dyne-review-contract.md`), so a listing needs a
rule for when a request counts as closed.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] `forge.py --forge none list --state open|merged|all` returns rows built
      from `refs/notes/review` requests and branch state, in the same row
      shape as the gh backend, or the ticket records why it should not.
- [ ] The rule for open / merged / abandoned is written down and tested
      (merged = head is an ancestor of the base; an abandoned request is a
      recorded state, not inferred from the branch being missing).
- [ ] Exit-code contract kept: 0 answer, 2 could not look, 3 only when no
      notes ref exists at all; never an empty `[]` for "could not read".
- [ ] Ticket 1445's limitations section mentions the capability or its absence.
